{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd .. && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor it in s.get('interpretations',[]):\n    if it.get('id','').startswith('d4377935'):\n        print(it.get('rationale','')[-3000:])\n        print('--- LIMITS')\n        for l in it.get('limitations',[]): print('-',l)\n        print('--- CE')\n        for c in it.get('counterevidence',[]): print('-',c[:400])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":", which is exactly 128 batches, and NewRaft returned in 45 ms (parsed index 7, comparison holds), while 131073 entries, one entry further and therefore 129 batches, did not return within 5000 ms (parsed index 9, comparison violated). The same capacity limit therefore governs a replay whose entry count is sixteen times larger, so the boundary follows the batch count rather than the default entry count. A third configuration point, receipt 14286fc183ac4bbc92a41f73a49a57d9 (completed, tests_passed, exit 0), moves the batch size to its smallest legal value: with MaxAppendEntries=1 the same product predicts a boundary of 128 entries. Parsed index 0 records replay_entries=128, replay_batches=128 and staged_commit_index=128; parsed index 1 records returned=true in 1 ms. Parsed index 2 records replay_entries=129 and replay_batches=129; parsed index 3 records returned=false after 5004 ms with the error 'no return within observation window', fsm_applied_sampled=0 and one new goroutine inside processLogs. The stated product 128*MaxAppendEntries therefore holds at 1, 64 and 1024, a span of 1024 in the entry count at which the block appears, and the smallest case shows the block needs no large volume of data, only one batch more than the channel can hold. A real-prefix case inside the shortened-replay region, receipt 7ee579ae94bd4812beaa21ce1ca9bb2e (completed, tests_passed, exit 0), commits 200 commands, takes a snapshot, commits a second batch and shuts down, so the restart replays only the tail after the snapshot. Parsed index 0 records snapshot_index=202, staged_commit_index=400, last_log_index=402, replay_entries=198 and replay_batches=4; parsed index 1 records returned=true in 3 ms. Parsed index 2 records the same first batch and snapshot with a second batch of 8300, giving staged_commit_index=8501, last_log_index=8502, replay_entries=8299 and replay_batches=130; parsed index 3 records returned=false after 5000 ms with one new goroutine inside processLogs. The recovery region between a zero-length replay and the blocked case is therefore observed on real prefixes rather than inferred: when the snapshot leaves four batches the constructor returns, and when it leaves 130 it does not. Permanence, receipt fd88bc508e4f413393838b7d64657137 (completed, tests_passed, exit 0): the seeded boundary pair at the default batch size, watched for longer than any earlier receipt. Parsed index 0 records 8192 entries and replay_batches=128 with a ten-second window; parsed index 1 records returned=true in 5 ms. Parsed index 2 records 8193 entries and replay_batches=129 with a thirty-second window; parsed index 3 records returned=false after 30000 ms with one new goroutine inside processLogs. The blocked case therefore outlasts the control's completion by roughly five thousand times, which is evidence of a permanent stall rather than a slow replay, and the source-level argument about the absent consumer and the unreachable closer is no longer the only ground for that claim.\n--- LIMITS\n- The boundary is 128*MaxAppendEntries committed entries beyond the last snapshot, and it was exercised at three batch sizes: at 1 the boundary is 128 entries (129 blocked, 128 returned), at 64 it is 8192 entries (8193 blocked, 8192 returned) and at 1024 it is 131072 entries (131073 blocked, 131072 returned). Only three points were sampled, all with one entry per batch unit or a whole multiple of it, and the 128-slot fsmMutateCh capacity is not configurable, so no case varied it.\n- The seeded cases supply the commit-tracking store state directly. Five restarts rest on a real prefix: a single-node leader (staged commit 8300, last log 8302, because the leader stages the pre-dispatch commit index), a three-voter cluster follower in both the snapshot and no-snapshot cases (staged commit 8400, last log 8402), and the mid-run-snapshot prefixes at staged commits 400 and 8501 over a snapshot at 202.\n- The shortened-replay region is now measured on real prefixes at four and 130 batches, but no real prefix was restarted with a tail between five and 129 batches; the seeded nine-batch case covers that middle range, and the real-prefix evidence brackets it at both ends rather than sampling inside it.\n- fsm_applied_sampled is one sample at the end of each window and was 0 in every case, including the returning controls where runFSM had not yet applied anything, so it is not used as evidence of completion.\n- Out of scope here: whether the installSnapshot and restoreUserSnapshot sends to the same channel can block indefinitely on a running node, where runFSM is already active and would provide backpressure rather than a permanent block.\n- The longest observation is thirty seconds. A replay that would eventually complete after that remains indistinguishable from one that never completes, although the control of the same shape finishes in milliseconds and the goroutine remains parked in processLogs throughout.\n--- CE\n- RestoreCommittedLogs is not part of DefaultConfig (config.go:329-341 leaves it false), and with the option disabled restoreFromCommittedLogs returns immediately (api.go:725-727), so default-configured nodes cannot reach the blocked path.\n- A snapshot at or near the commit index removes the replay entirely: parsed index 3 in receipt d61420dbc49a4072937b040aac55a51c and the short case of receipt 7ee579ae94bd4812beaa21ce1ca9bb2e restarted real prefixes with a small enough tail that NewRaft returned, so snapshot cadence bounds exposure to this path.\n- A larger MaxAppendEntries moves the boundary: parsed index 5 of receipt 87aea51bbd8a4b579f138f2c4986343a completed the very state that hangs at the default batch size, so a deployment that raises MaxAppendEntries enough for 128*MaxAppendEntries to cover its snapshot lag does not encounter the block.\n- The trigger observations are bounded windows, now including a thirty-second one in receipt fd88bc508e4f413393838b7d64657137, but still finite runs; permanence is claimed from the source-level absence of any consumer before NewRaft returns, from shutdownCh having no closer outside Shutdown on the returned *Raft, and from the blocked case outlasting the 128-batch control's five-millisecond completio\n- No incorrect commit, applied entry, vote or membership decision was observed in any case; the evidence contradicts only startup progress.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
