ts down, so the\n//     restart replays only the tail after the snapshot.\n//   * Three tails are measured at the default MaxAppendEntries of 64: about\n//     fifty FSM batches, which is inside the range that earlier receipts only\n//     covered with a seeded store state; exactly one hundred and twenty eight\n//     batches, the channel capacity itself, measured on a large real tail so a\n//     slow replay cannot be mistaken for a capacity effect; and one entry past\n//     that, which the seeded boundary says must not return.\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"runtime\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Submitted an experiment request for the startup committed-log replay investigation. The new harness, exp_restore_midband_test.go, runs three restarts of retained single-node cluster state with RestoreCommittedLogs enabled and the default MaxAppendEntries of 64, where each cluster commits 200 commands, snapshots, commits a second batch and shuts down, so only the post-snapshot tail is replayed. Local verification shows a 50-batch tail (3198 entries) returns in 1 ms, a 128-batch tail (8192 entries, exactly the fsmMutateCh capacity) returns in 3 ms, and 129 batches (8194 entries) does not return within 20 s with one goroutine parked in processLogs. This fills the account's stated gap that no real prefix had been restarted with a tail between 5 and 129 batches, and it brackets the capacity boundary on cluster-produced state with the tail held fixed to a single entry, so the trigger can no longer be attributed to replay volume or slowness. The request anchors prerequisites on the three case events and asserts returned == true on each result event; it validates against submission.schema.json.\"}"}}
